\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Project Context}

In this stage, the focus has shifted towards creating a robust, distributed architecture capable of handling large-scale data processing for a search engine. We maintain the core microservices (Ingestion, Indexing, Search) but deploy them in a clustered environment ensuring scalability and fault tolerance.

\begin{successbox}[Key Objectives]
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Scalability}: Design a system that can scale horizontally across multiple nodes
    \item \textbf{High Availability}: Eliminate single points of failure through clustering
    \item \textbf{Performance}: Optimize search and indexing operations using distributed caching
    \item \textbf{Fault Tolerance}: Ensure system resilience through redundancy and failover mechanisms
    \item \textbf{Real-World Deployment}: Implement and test on actual laboratory infrastructure
\end{itemize}
\end{successbox}

\subsection{System Overview}

The Book Search Platform is a distributed system designed to:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Discover} books from Project Gutenberg's public domain collection
    \item \textbf{Download} and store books in a distributed datalake
    \item \textbf{Index} book content for efficient full-text search
    \item \textbf{Search} across the indexed corpus with low latency
    \item \textbf{Scale} horizontally to handle increasing data volumes
\end{enumerate}

\section{System Architecture}

This project implements a distributed architecture designed for scalability and fault tolerance.

\subsection{Distributed Messaging: ActiveMQ Artemis}

\begin{techbox}[Message Broker Architecture]
A core component of our architecture is the message broker. We have migrated to using \textbf{ActiveMQ Artemis} configured as a high-availability cluster.

We have deployed a \textbf{3-node ActiveMQ Artemis cluster}. The nodes (\texttt{node1}, \texttt{node2}, \texttt{node3}) work together to form a distributed messaging fabric. \textbf{ActiveMQ Artemis} was chosen for its high performance and advanced features, as detailed in the official documentation [3].
\end{techbox}

\vspace{0.3cm}

This setup provides several key advantages:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{High Efficiency and Load Distribution}: By redistributing messages across the cluster, we ensure that no single broker is a bottleneck. Consumers can connect to any node in the cluster, and messages are efficiently routed to where they are needed. This redistribution mechanism optimizes the consumption of messages by the worker machines (Ingestion and Indexing services).

    \item \textbf{Redistribution Configuration}: The key mechanism enabling this distributed behavior is the \texttt{redistribution-delay} setting within the \texttt{broker.xml} configuration files. By setting this parameter to \texttt{0} inside the \texttt{<address-settings>} block, we ensure that messages are immediately redistributed to other nodes if there are no local consumers available, maximizing throughput and minimizing latency.

    \item \textbf{High Availability and Fault Tolerance}: The cluster architecture avoids a \textit{Single Point of Failure (SPOF)}. If one machine or broker node goes down, the other nodes continue to operate, ensuring the workflow is not interrupted. The system is highly distributed, meaning the failure of a single component does not stop the entire data processing pipeline.
\end{enumerate}

The services connect to the broker using a failover transport string, enabling them to automatically reconnect to available nodes in case of network issues or node failure.

\subsection{Data Management: Hazelcast and MongoDB}

\begin{infobox}[Hybrid Data Strategy]
To optimize performance without sacrificing data integrity, we implemented a hybrid data strategy combining in-memory caching with persistent storage:
\end{infobox}

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{5cm}|p{5cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{Role} & \textbf{Key Features} \\
\hline
\textbf{Hazelcast} & Distributed In-Memory Cache &
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, nosep]
    \item Ultra-low latency ($<$ 1ms)
    \item Distributed data grid
    \item Automatic partitioning
    \item Cluster-wide synchronization
\end{itemize} \\
\hline
\textbf{MongoDB} & Persistent Data Store &
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, nosep]
    \item Authoritative source of truth
    \item Durable storage
    \item Book metadata and references
    \item Survives system restarts
\end{itemize} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Data layer components and their responsibilities}
\end{table}

\begin{warningbox}[Important Note]
\textbf{Hazelcast does NOT replace MongoDB}. Hazelcast provides speed for active processing and caching, while MongoDB ensures data durability and persistence. Both components work together to provide optimal performance and reliability.
\end{warningbox}


\subsubsection{High Availability and Distributed Service Deployment}

The core microservices (\textbf{Crawler}, \textbf{Indexer}, and \textbf{Search}) are deployed across all three cluster nodes. This strategy ensures the system is inherently \textbf{highly available} and \textbf{distributed}.

\begin{techbox}[Service Replication Strategy]
\textbf{Goal}: Achieve redundancy and load distribution across the cluster.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Deployment Model}: Each node (\texttt{Node 1}, \texttt{Node 2}, \texttt{Node 3}) runs instances of the \texttt{Crawler}, \texttt{Indexer}, and \texttt{Search} services.
    \item \textbf{High Availability (HA)}: In the event of a machine failure (e.g., \texttt{Node 1} goes down), the processing load is instantly taken over by the redundant services running on the remaining operational machines (\texttt{Node 2} and \texttt{Node 3}).
    \item \textbf{Load Distribution}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Search Service}: Incoming requests are distributed by the Nginx Load Balancer (running on \texttt{Node 2}) across all available instances of the \texttt{Search} service in the cluster.
        \item \textbf{Crawler/Indexer Services}: These services consume events from the ActiveMQ Artemis cluster, whose message redistribution mechanism ensures that messages are processed efficiently by any available worker instance across the three nodes.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
This architecture ensures that the failure of a single physical machine does not result in a system outage, fulfilling the key objective of fault tolerance through redundancy.
\end{techbox}

