\subsection{Deduplication Mechanism}

\begin{successbox}[Efficiency Optimization]
The \textbf{Crawler Service} implements an intelligent deduplication mechanism to avoid re-downloading and re-indexing books that are already present in the datalake.

\textbf{Implementation}: Simple file existence check before initiating download

\textbf{Benefits}:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item Prevents redundant network usage
    \item Saves processing power (CPU)
    \item Reduces storage I/O operations
    \item Ensures only new content is processed
\end{itemize}
\end{successbox}

\subsection{Task Scheduler for Periodic Crawling}

To avoid relying on manual execution of the Crawler service and to keep the datalake continuously up to date, a \textbf{task scheduler} was added to the architecture.

\begin{successbox}[Automated Book Discovery]
The task scheduler is responsible for periodically triggering new crawling jobs without human intervention. Its main responsibilities are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Periodic execution}: automatically enqueue new crawl tasks at configurable intervals (e.g., every few minutes or hours).
    \item \textbf{Integration with the message broker}: instead of downloading books directly, scheduled tasks publish crawl requests to ActiveMQ Artemis, so that worker instances of the Crawler can process them asynchronously.
    \item \textbf{Datastore refresh}: ensures that newly available books in Project Gutenberg are gradually discovered and ingested into the datalake and index.
    \item \textbf{Operational robustness}: reduces the risk of forgetting to execute manual scripts and provides a more realistic, production-like ingestion model.
\end{itemize}
\end{successbox}

From the perspective of the rest of the system, the scheduler behaves as another producer of crawl events. This keeps the design loosely coupled: the Crawler service and the downstream Indexer do not need to know whether a request was created manually or by the task scheduler.


\section{Background and Investigation Work}

\subsection{Kubernetes: Local Cluster Deployment with kind}

As part of the investigation, Kubernetes was studied using the official kind documentation [1], and the internal architecture of Kubernetes was analyzed through the official project documentation [2].
A significant effort was dedicated to understand and configure Kubernetes using \texttt{kind} (Kubernetes IN Docker) [1]. Although Kubernetes was not required for the final deployment of the microservices, it was intentionally explored due to its relevance in large-scale distributed architectures.

This research complements the main system development by offering insights into how the ingestion, indexing, and search services could operate inside a realistic distributed environment. Kubernetes provides features such as declarative deployments, load balancing, automatic recovery, and namespace isolation.

\subsubsection*{Overview and Motivation}

Docker provides lightweight containers, but Kubernetes extends these capabilities to full orchestration. Benefits include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Automated Pod recovery.
    \item Declarative configuration with YAML.
    \item Load balancing.
    \item Autoscaling capabilities.
    \item Namespaces for isolation.
    \item Multi-node cluster simulation with \texttt{kind}.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Project Structure and Manifests}

The Kubernetes configuration used during the investigation was organized in \texttt{cluster/manifests}, divided per microservice:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{indexing/}
    \item \texttt{ingestion/}
    \item \texttt{search/}
\end{itemize}

Each included:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Namespace
    \item Deployment
    \item Service
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Namespaces}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: indexing
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Deployments}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: indexing
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: indexing
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: indexing
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: indexing
        image: localhost:5001/indexing:0.0.1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Services}

\begin{lstlisting}
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: indexing
spec:
  selector:
    app: indexing
  ports:
  - protocol: TCP
    port: 80
    targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Cluster Creation with kind}

\begin{lstlisting}
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
nodes:
  - role: control-plane
  - role: worker
  - role: worker
  - role: worker
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}
kind create cluster --config=cluster/cluster-config.yaml
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Local Docker Registry Integration}

\begin{lstlisting}
docker build -t localhost:5001/mi-app:latest .
docker push localhost:5001/mi-app:latest
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Verification and Cleanup}

\begin{lstlisting}
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A
kind delete cluster
docker rm -f kind-registry
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection*{Conclusion}

This Kubernetes investigation provides a foundation for future scalability and real-world deployment strategies.
