\subsection{Container Preparation and Registry Publishing for Project Deployment}

\begin{techbox}[Multi-Architecture Build Strategy]
To ensure consistent execution across different laboratory machines and hardware architectures, all microservice images were built using \texttt{docker buildx} with multi-architecture support.
\end{techbox}

\vspace{0.3cm}

Specifically, the \texttt{--platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64} flag was used to generate cross-platform images that run seamlessly on both:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{x86\_64 servers}: Standard Intel/AMD processors
    \item \textbf{ARM-based systems}: Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3) and ARM servers
\end{itemize}

\begin{infobox}[Docker Hub Repository]
All images were published publicly to Docker Hub under the account \textbf{giselabcr8888} using the \texttt{--push} flag. This ensures that both architectures, along with their manifest list, are available for transparent pull operations on any machine.

\textbf{Repository URL}: \url{https://hub.docker.com/repositories/giselabcr8888}
\end{infobox}

\subsubsection{Benefits of Multi-Architecture Publishing}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{10cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Benefit} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
\textbf{Reproducibility} & Ensures consistent behavior across all laboratory environments \\
\hline
\textbf{Efficiency} & Avoids the need to rebuild images on each machine \\
\hline
\textbf{Compatibility} & Guarantees compatibility with both AMD64 and ARM64 architectures \\
\hline
\textbf{Centralization} & Provides a single, validated, publicly available image source for deployment \\
\hline
\textbf{Version Control} & Enables proper versioning and rollback capabilities \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Advantages of multi-architecture container publishing}
\end{table}

\section{Laboratory Deployment Configuration}

\subsection{Docker Compose Files for Multi-Node Deployment}

To facilitate the deployment and testing of the distributed system in the laboratory environment,
three separate Docker Compose configuration files were created, one for each node in the cluster:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{docker-compose-node1.yaml}
    \item \texttt{docker-compose-node2.yaml}
    \item \texttt{docker-compose-node3.yaml}
\end{itemize}

Each file is specifically configured for its corresponding node, with appropriate IP addresses,
service configurations, and network settings tailored to the laboratory infrastructure.

\subsection{Deployment Commands}

To execute and start the services on each node, the following commands must be run on the
respective machines:

\begin{verbatim}
# On Node 1
docker compose -f docker-compose-node1.yaml up -d

# On Node 2
docker compose -f docker-compose-node2.yaml up -d

# On Node 3
docker compose -f docker-compose-node3.yaml up -d
\end{verbatim}

The \texttt{-d} flag ensures that the containers run in detached mode, allowing them to
operate in the background.

\subsection{Updated Docker Images}

New versions of the microservice images were built and published to Docker Hub \href{https://hub.docker.com/repositories/giselabcr8888}{(https://hub.docker.com/repositories/giselabcr8888)} to support
the laboratory deployment. The following images and their versions are now available:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Crawler Service}: \texttt{giselabcr8888/crawler:1.2.0}
    \item \textbf{Indexer Service}: \texttt{giselabcr8888/indexer:1.1.0}
    \item \textbf{Search Service}: \texttt{giselabcr8888/search:1.2.1}
\end{itemize}

These images incorporate all the necessary configurations and optimizations for distributed
execution in the cluster environment, including proper Hazelcast client configuration,
ActiveMQ cluster connectivity, and MongoDB integration.

\subsection{GitHub Laboratory Branch}

A dedicated branch named \textbf{lab} was created in the GitHub repository specifically for
laboratory testing and deployment. This branch contains all the configurations, scripts, and
documentation required to successfully deploy and test the system in the laboratory environment.

The branch can be accessed at:

\begin{center}
\texttt{https://github.com/The-almost-honor-students/Stage\_3/tree/lab}
\end{center}

\subsection{Transversal Services Architecture}

\textbf{Transversal services} are those common components that are shared across the cluster
and must be available to all nodes. In our architecture, these include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{MongoDB}: The persistent data storage layer.
    \item \textbf{Nginx}: The reverse proxy and HTTP load balancer.
    \item \textbf{Prometheus}: The metrics backend that scrapes and stores time-series data from all microservices.
    \item \textbf{Jaeger}: The distributed tracing backend used to visualize and analyse request propagation across services.
    \item \textbf{OpenTelemetry Collector}: The ingestion and routing pipeline that receives telemetry (metrics, logs, traces) from all services and forwards them to Prometheus, Loki and Jaeger.
    \item \textbf{Loki}: The centralized log aggregation system for querying container logs.
    \item \textbf{Grafana}: The unified observability dashboard where metrics (Prometheus), logs (Loki) and traces (Jaeger) are visualized together.
\end{itemize}

The transversal components are deployed through the
\texttt{transversal\_services/docker-compose.yaml} file, which centralizes the monitoring and
observability stack. All microservices deployed across the cluster communicate with these
components to report their operational data.

For the laboratory environment, all transversal services were deployed on \textbf{Node 2}. This node therefore hosts:

\begin{itemize}
    \item MongoDB
    \item Nginx Load Balancer
    \item Prometheus
    \item Jaeger
    \item Loki
    \item Grafana
    \item OpenTelemetry Collector
\end{itemize}

This ensures that every node has access to the same monitoring and persistence infrastructure.

\subsubsection*{Deployment Example: Node 2 Configuration}

\begin{verbatim}
# Start transversal services (MongoDB, Nginx, Prometheus, Jaeger, Loki, Grafana, OTel Collector)
docker compose -f transversal_services/docker-compose.yaml up -d

# Start Node 2 microservices (Search, Indexer, etc.)
docker compose -f docker-compose-node2.yaml up -d
\end{verbatim}

Deploying all observability components together allows telemetry (metrics, logs, traces) to be
visualized centrally through Grafana.

\subsubsection*{Deployment Example: Node 2 Configuration}

In our testing scenario, \textbf{Node 2} was configured to run both its own node-specific services
and the transversal services. This required executing two separate Docker Compose commands on the
same machine:

\begin{verbatim}
# First, start the transversal services (MongoDB and Nginx)
docker compose -f transversal_services/docker-compose.yaml up -d

# Then, start the Node 2 specific services (Search, Indexer, etc.)
docker compose -f docker-compose-node2.yaml up -d
\end{verbatim}

This dual deployment approach on Node 2 provides several advantages:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Centralized Data Access}: All nodes can connect to the MongoDB instance
    running on Node 2, ensuring consistent data access across the cluster.

    \item \textbf{Load Balancing}: The Nginx reverse proxy on Node 2 can distribute incoming
    search requests across all available search service instances in the cluster.

    \item \textbf{Resource Optimization}: By consolidating transversal services on a single
    node, we reduce redundancy and optimize resource utilization across the cluster.

    \item \textbf{Simplified Configuration}: Having a designated node for shared services
    simplifies the network configuration and service discovery for other nodes.
\end{itemize}

The \texttt{transversal\_services/docker-compose.yaml} file contains the service definitions
for \textbf{MongoDB}, \textbf{Nginx}, \textbf{Prometheus}, \textbf{Jaeger}, the
\textbf{OpenTelemetry Collector} and \textbf{Loki}, while
\texttt{docker-compose-node2.yaml} contains the node-specific
services (crawler, indexer, search, and ActiveMQ broker).


\subsection{Observability and Monitoring with OpenTelemetry}

To monitor the behaviour of the platform in a consistent way, we use \textbf{OpenTelemetry}
as a technology-agnostic observability layer. Instead of tying the microservices to a
specific monitoring vendor or library, each service emits telemetry data (metrics, traces
and logs) in a standard format that is understood by the \textbf{OpenTelemetry Collector}.

The OpenTelemetry Collector runs as part of the transversal services defined in
\texttt{transversal\_services/docker-compose.yaml}. From there, it:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Receives telemetry from the Crawler, Indexer and Search services.
    \item Normalizes and processes that data in a single place.
    \item Exports \textbf{metrics} to Prometheus, \textbf{traces} to Jaeger and \textbf{logs} to Loki.
\end{itemize}

Using OpenTelemetry has two main advantages for this project:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Technology agnostic}: we do not depend on a single monitoring technology. If we decide to change the backend (for example, use another metrics system), we can reconfigure the Collector without modifying the microservices.
    \item \textbf{Low coupling}: services only know that they send telemetry using OpenTelemetry. All the knowledge about concrete tools (Prometheus, Jaeger, Loki) is isolated in the Collector and the transversal services Docker Compose file.
\end{itemize}

This setup gives us a simple but complete observability stack, where metrics, traces and logs
are collected for the whole cluster through the Nginx entrypoint and the internal
communication between services.

\section{Testing and Verification}

\begin{infobox}[Quality Assurance Strategy]
To ensure the reliability, correctness, and performance of the distributed system, a comprehensive testing strategy was implemented covering unit tests, integration tests.
\end{infobox}

\subsubsection*{Grafana as Unified Observability Dashboard}

All telemetry exported through the OpenTelemetry Collector is made accessible through
\textbf{Grafana}. This interface consolidates:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Prometheus metrics (CPU, memory, request latency, request throughput)
    \item Jaeger traces (distributed spans across Nginx → Search → Indexer)
    \item Loki logs (service logs for debugging)
\end{itemize}

Grafana acts as the single entry point for observability, enabling correlation between
latency spikes, specific traces and associated logs.

\subsection{Unit Testing}

\begin{techbox}[Core Logic Verification]
Unit tests were essential for verifying the core logic of individual components in isolation:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TextTokenizerTest}: Verifies that book content is correctly split into tokens (terms) for indexing. This is critical for search engine accuracy.

    \item \textbf{GutenbergHeaderSerializerTest}: Ensures that metadata (title, author, language) is correctly extracted from raw Project Gutenberg files.
\end{itemize}
\end{techbox}

\subsection{Integration and Acceptance Testing}

\begin{successbox}[End-to-End Validation]
Acceptance tests guarantee that microservices interact correctly:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{IndexingApiAcceptanceTest}: Validates the end-to-end functionality of the indexing service, ensuring that books sent to the API are correctly processed and stored.
\end{itemize}
\end{successbox}

\subsection{Load Testing with Locust}

\begin{warningbox}[Performance Validation]
To evaluate the system's performance under realistic user load conditions and measure key metrics such as latency, throughput, and response times, comprehensive load testing using \textbf{Locust} is essential. This test was specifically configured to direct traffic to the Nginx Load Balancer running on port \texttt{8000}.
\end{warningbox}

\subsubsection{Test Configuration and Locustfile}

The test simulated \textbf{300 concurrent users} making search requests. The user behavior was defined in the following Python script (\texttt{locustfile.py}), utilizing the keywords \texttt{from locust import HttpUser, task, between} and \texttt{import random}:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, caption=Locustfile Configuration for Search User]
from locust import HttpUser, task, between
import random

class SearchUser(HttpUser):
    # Simulate a user thinking for 1-3 seconds between searches
    wait_time = between(1, 3)

    # Partial list of search terms used for random queries
    search_terms = [
        "love", "home", "happiness", "health", "money", "engineering", "computer",
        # ... (Lista completa de 100+ términos)
        "2020", "2022", "2023", "2024"
    ]

    @task
    def search_books(self):
        term = random.choice(self.search_terms)
        # Target: Nginx Load Balancer on port 8000
        self.client.get(f"/search?q={term}", name="/search")
\end{lstlisting}

The test was executed against the host $\texttt{http://localhost:8000/search}$, which is the endpoint exposed by the Nginx reverse proxy, responsible for distributing the load to the backend search services.

\subsubsection{Execution and Results}

The load test followed these steps, captured in the figures below:

\paragraph{Step 1: Starting Locust}
The test began by running the command \texttt{locust -f locustfile.py} from the terminal, which initializes the Locust web interface on port 8089.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{iniciar_locust.png}
    \caption{Starting the Locust web server on port 8089.}
    \label{fig:locust_start}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Step 2: Test Configuration}
The test was configured via the web interface to simulate $\mathbf{300}$ concurrent users, with a ramp-up rate of 1 user per second, targeting the Nginx Load Balancer.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.8\textwidth]{configuration_locust.png}
    \caption{Locust test configuration, targeting the Nginx Load Balancer on \texttt{http://localhost:8000/search}.}
    \label{fig:locust_config}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Step 3: Initial Statistics (Spawning Phase)}
As users were generated, the statistics tab provided real-time metrics, showing the system handling the initial traffic and reporting key response times (Median, P95) and the initial failure rate.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{statistics_locust.png}
    \caption{Initial statistics view during the Spawning phase (11 users active).}
    \label{fig:locust_initial_stats}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Step 4: Real-time Performance Charts}
The Charts tab showed the dynamic evolution of the system's performance metrics as the user count increased.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{charts_locust_running.png}
    \caption{Locust charts showing the evolution of Requests per Second (RPS) and response times (p50, p95) during the test run.}
    \label{fig:locust_running_charts}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Step 5: Final Results at Peak Concurrency (300 Users)}
The full-duration charts reveal the system's behavior up to and at the peak load of $\mathbf{300}$ concurrent users. The data highlights a maximum throughput of approximately 60 RPS, followed by an increase in the 95th percentile (P95) response time, indicating potential contention or resource saturation under maximum stress.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{total_requests_per_second_1765460149.69.png}
    \caption{Locust charts after reaching 300 users, detailing the evolution of concurrent users, RPS, and latency throughout the test.}
    \label{fig:locust_final_charts_full}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{final_300_users.png}
    \caption{Final interface view confirming 300 active users and the recorded performance metrics.}
    \label{fig:locust_final_stats}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Load Testing Conclusion}

The Locust load test successfully validated the configuration of the Nginx Load Balancer to handle concurrent traffic on port 8000. The test provided crucial performance metrics, specifically demonstrating the system's capacity to sustain a certain level of throughput before significant latency increases were observed under the full load of 300 users. These results are essential for identifying optimization opportunities in the backend services to improve resilience and reduce P95 latency under high concurrency.

\section{Conclusions and Future Work}

\begin{successbox}[Project Achievement Summary]
This project successfully demonstrates the design, implementation, and deployment of a production-ready distributed book search platform using modern Big Data technologies and architectural patterns.
\end{successbox}

\subsection{Technical Achievements}

\subsubsection{Architecture and Design}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|l|p{10cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{Achievement} & \textbf{Implementation} \\
\hline
\textbf{Microservices Architecture} & Successfully decomposed the system into three independent, scalable services (Crawler, Indexer, Search) \\
\hline
\textbf{Event-Driven Design} & Implemented asynchronous communication using ActiveMQ Artemis with message redistribution \\
\hline
\textbf{High Availability} & Deployed 3-node ActiveMQ cluster eliminating single points of failure \\
\hline
\textbf{Distributed Caching} & Integrated Hazelcast for ultra-low latency ($<$ 1ms) data access \\
\hline
\textbf{Persistent Storage} & MongoDB as authoritative data source with durability guarantees \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Key architectural achievements}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Performance Optimizations}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Deduplication Mechanism}: Prevents redundant downloads and indexing operations
    \item \textbf{Cache-First Strategy}: Minimizes database queries for frequently accessed data
    \item \textbf{Load Balancing}: Nginx distributes requests across multiple service instances
    \item \textbf{Horizontal Scalability}: Services can scale independently based on demand
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Deployment and DevOps}

\begin{techbox}[Multi-Architecture Container Strategy]
The project demonstrates professional DevOps practices:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Multi-Architecture Images}: Built for both AMD64 and ARM64 platforms
    \item \textbf{Public Registry}: All images published to Docker Hub for easy deployment
    \item \textbf{Version Control}: Dedicated \texttt{lab} branch for laboratory testing
    \item \textbf{Infrastructure as Code}: Complete Docker Compose configurations for all nodes
\end{itemize}
\end{techbox}

\subsection{Testing and Quality Assurance}

The comprehensive testing strategy ensures system reliability:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Unit Tests}: Verify core logic components (tokenization, metadata extraction)
    \item \textbf{Integration Tests}: Validate end-to-end service interactions
    \item \textbf{Load Testing}: Locust scenarios for realistic user load simulation
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Lessons Learned}

\begin{infobox}[Key Insights]
\textbf{Kubernetes Investigation}: While not required for final deployment, the Kubernetes exploration with \texttt{kind} provided valuable insights into container orchestration, declarative deployments, and production-grade cluster management.

\textbf{Message Broker Configuration}: The \texttt{redistribution-delay=0} setting in ActiveMQ Artemis was crucial for optimal message distribution across cluster nodes.

\textbf{Hybrid Data Strategy}: Combining Hazelcast (speed) with MongoDB (durability) proved essential for balancing performance and data integrity.
\end{infobox}

\subsection{Future Work}

\begin{warningbox}[Potential Enhancements]
The following improvements could further enhance the system:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Kubernetes Production Deployment}: Migrate from Docker Compose to Kubernetes for advanced orchestration features (auto-scaling, self-healing, rolling updates)

    \item \textbf{Observability Stack}: Extend the current observability stack (OpenTelemetry Collector + Prometheus + Jaeger + Loki) by:

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Adding \textbf{Grafana} dashboards for metrics and traces.
        \item Defining alerts based on Prometheus metrics (error rates, latency, saturation).
        \item Improving log exploration by integrating Loki dashboards in Grafana.
        \item Refining sampling strategies and retention policies for production-like scenarios.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Advanced Search Features}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Fuzzy search and spell correction
        \item Relevance ranking algorithms
        \item Faceted search capabilities
        \item Search result highlighting
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Data Analytics Pipeline}: Integrate Apache Spark for:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Book popularity analysis
        \item Search pattern analytics
        \item Recommendation engine
        \item Usage statistics and reporting
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Security Enhancements}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item API authentication and authorization
        \item TLS/SSL encryption for inter-service communication
        \item Secrets management with HashiCorp Vault
        \item Rate limiting and DDoS protection
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Disaster Recovery}: Implement backup strategies and cross-region replication

    \item \textbf{CI/CD Pipeline}: Automated testing and deployment with GitHub Actions or Jenkins
\end{enumerate}
\end{warningbox}

\subsection{Final Remarks}

\begin{successbox}[Conclusion]
This project successfully demonstrates the practical application of Big Data technologies and distributed systems principles. The architecture is scalable, fault-tolerant, and production-ready, showcasing modern best practices in microservices design, event-driven architecture, and containerized deployment.

The system handles the complete lifecycle of book data: from discovery and ingestion through Project Gutenberg, to distributed indexing with Hazelcast and MongoDB, to efficient search capabilities with caching optimization. The multi-node cluster deployment with ActiveMQ Artemis ensures high availability and eliminates single points of failure.

The knowledge and experience gained from this project are directly applicable to real-world Big Data systems and provide a solid foundation for future work in distributed computing and large-scale data processing.
\end{successbox}


\section{Laboratory Procedure Guide: Distributed Deployment and Testing}

\begin{infobox}[Cluster Node Configuration]
The distributed system is deployed across three laboratory nodes. The final digit of the IP determines the node number.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Node} & \textbf{IP Address} & \textbf{Role} \\
\hline
\textbf{Node 1} & \texttt{10.26.14.221} & Microservices \\
\hline
\textbf{Node 2} & \texttt{10.26.14.222} & Microservices + Transversal (MongoDB, Nginx...) \\
\hline
\textbf{Node 3} & \texttt{10.26.14.223} & Microservices \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{infobox}



\subsection{Environment Setup}

\subsubsection{Python Environment Preparation for Load Testing}
Before executing Locust, a Python virtual environment must be configured and necessary dependencies activated. This is only required on the machine designated for running Locust (typically one of the nodes or a client).

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Create the virtual environment:
    \begin{lstlisting}
python3 -m venv Stage_3
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item Activate the virtual environment:
    \begin{lstlisting}
source Stage_3/bin/activate
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item Install Locust and dependencies:
    \begin{lstlisting}
pip install locust
    \end{lstlisting}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Service Deployment}

\subsubsection{Node-Specific Services Deployment}
On each of the three cluster nodes, execute the \texttt{docker compose} command to deploy the microservices specific to that node (\texttt{crawler}, \texttt{indexer}, \texttt{search}, ActiveMQ).

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{On Node 1 (\texttt{10.26.14.221}):}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker compose -f docker-compose-node1.yaml up -d
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{On Node 2 (\texttt{10.26.14.222}):}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker compose -f docker-compose-node2.yaml up -d
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{On Node 3 (\texttt{10.26.14.223}):}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker compose -f docker-compose-node3.yaml up -d
    \end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Transversal Services Deployment (Node 2 Only)}
The shared infrastructure services (\textbf{MongoDB} and the \textbf{Nginx Load Balancer}) are implemented centrally on Node 2.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Navigate to the transversal services directory:
    \begin{lstlisting}
cd transversal_services
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item Execute the deployment of \texttt{mongodb} and \texttt{nginx}:
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker compose up -d
    \end{lstlisting}
\end{enumerate}
At this point, all microservices and transversal services should be deployed and running across the cluster.

\subsection{Datalake Configuration and Synchronization}

\subsubsection{Execution Policy Configuration (Windows/PowerShell)}
Before executing the configuration script, the PowerShell execution policy must be modified to allow the running of local scripts.

\begin{lstlisting}
Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope Process
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection{Execution of the Synchronization Script}
Execute the \texttt{configure\_sizing.ps1} script to prepare the data volumes and ensure the datalake (the shared book folder) is correctly configured and visible to all nodes for replication purposes.

\begin{lstlisting}
./configure_sizing.ps1
\end{lstlisting}

\subsubsection{Accepting Datalake Connections}
Ensure that the network or firewall configurations on all three machines allow the necessary connections for sharing the datalake folder, so that all containers can access the books and cluster members (Hazelcast and ActiveMQ) can communicate effectively.

\subsection{System Verification and Monitoring}

After deploying and configuring the services, their correct operation is verified by reviewing the container logs.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Verify Crawler Logs:}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker logs <crawler_container_id> -f
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{Verify Indexer Logs:}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker logs <indexer_container_id> -f
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{Verify Search Service Logs:}
    \begin{lstlisting}
docker logs <search_container_id> -f
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{Verify MongoDB Express:} Access the MongoDB Express interface to verify the persistence of metadata and the index.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Observability Verification: Metrics, Traces and Logs}

Once the transversal services are deployed on Node 2, it is essential to verify that the
monitoring stack is functioning correctly. The observability pipeline consists of:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Prometheus} – collects metrics from microservices.
    \item \textbf{Jaeger} – stores and displays distributed traces.
    \item \textbf{Loki} – aggregates logs from all containers.
    \item \textbf{Grafana} – unified dashboard for visualizing metrics, traces and logs.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{Accessing Grafana}

Grafana runs at:

\begin{center}
\texttt{http://<node2-ip>:3000}
\end{center}

Example: \texttt{http://10.26.14.222:3000}

Default login:

\begin{itemize}
    \item User: \textbf{admin}
    \item Password: \textbf{admin}
\end{itemize}

Three data sources appear preconfigured:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Prometheus} (metrics)
    \item \textbf{Loki} (logs)
    \item \textbf{Jaeger} (traces)
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection*{What Must Be Validated}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Metrics}: Prometheus panels show activity from Search, Crawler, Indexer.
    \item \textbf{Traces}: Jaeger shows multi-service traces from the \texttt{/search} endpoint.
    \item \textbf{Logs}: Loki correctly displays logs from all microservices.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Load Testing with Locust}

To evaluate the cluster's performance under stress, Locust is used to simulate high concurrency directed at the Nginx load balancer.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Navigate to the load-testing folder:
    \begin{lstlisting}
cd load-testing
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item Run Locust:
    \begin{lstlisting}
locust -f locustfile.py
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item Access the Locust web interface on port \texttt{8089} of the host machine (e.g., \texttt{http://localhost:8089}).
    \item Configure and run the load test:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Number of Users:} $\mathbf{500}$ or more (to stress the system).
        \item \textbf{Host:} $\texttt{http://localhost:8000/search}$ (the Nginx endpoint).
    \end{itemize}
    \item Monitor key metrics such as Requests/s and the $\mathbf{95^{th}}$ Percentile (\textbf{P95}) latency.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Functional Search Service Test}

Perform a manual search test using a browser or a tool like \texttt{curl} or Postman, directed at the Nginx load balancer.

\begin{lstlisting}
curl http://localhost:8000/search?q=Love
\end{lstlisting}
Verify that the response is correct and originated from one of the Search Services in the cluster.

\subsection{Fault Tolerance Test (Failover)}

To verify the High Availability and fault tolerance of the distributed system:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Select a node (e.g., Node 1) and stop its microservice containers:
    \begin{lstlisting}
# On Node 1 machine
docker compose -f docker-compose-node1.yaml stop
    \end{lstlisting}
    \item \textbf{Verification:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Observe the logs of the remaining nodes (Node 2 and 3) to confirm the ActiveMQ and Hazelcast reconnections.
        \item Repeat the functional search test (\texttt{/search?q=Love}) to confirm that the system remains operational without interruptions, with traffic successfully being redirected to the remaining nodes.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

